function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests running-statistics in streaming data and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- Running median with two heaps; balance invariant; tie handling for even n.
- Complexity: O(log n) per insert, O(1) median; memory O(n).

## Code Example
```python
import heapq
class MedianMaint:
    def __init__(self):
        self.L = []  # max-heap via negatives
        self.R = []  # min-heap
    def add(self, x):
        if not self.L or x <= -self.L[0]:
            heapq.heappush(self.L, -x)
        else:
            heapq.heappush(self.R, x)
        if len(self.L) > len(self.R) + 1:
            heapq.heappush(self.R, -heapq.heappop(self.L))
        elif len(self.R) > len(self.L):
            heapq.heappush(self.L, -heapq.heappop(self.R))
    def median(self):
        if len(self.L) > len(self.R):
            return -self.L[0]
        return (-self.L[0] + self.R[0]) / 2
```

